the adapter refuses before attaching, announcing or leasing          (G3)\n  the connector refuses before it observes                             (C4)\n  publish reads under the deadline it advertises                       (C5)\n  every command that registers --deadline binds it                     (G2)\n  the batch fixture no longer assumes a fast resident                  (G1)\n  cmd/gs's TestMain clears the ambient variable                        (R1)\n\nG2 deserves a note. The mutation that survived two heads — gs promise resolving\nthe deadline and discarding the context — is dead, and\nTestEveryAppendingCommandBindsItsDeadline still passes under it. That is exactly\nthe gap: the runtime test proves resolution, the AST gate proves binding, and\nonly the second one catches it. The refusal names the repair in the author's own\nterms. It is the smallest thing that could have worked and it is in the idiom the\nrepository already had.\n\nARTIFACT SET: all thirty-three stand at c92b20cc, one per changed path, each\neffective and none stale, and the set is exactly git diff --name-only\nmain..c92b20cc. I checked every one.\n\nA process note that is not about the head: the scratch list at\n/tmp/gitseq-deadline-lane/artifact-list.txt still holds the thirty-two artifacts\nof ef555b8e. A reviewer who followed it would file a verdict citing retired\nartifacts at the wrong commit. I took the set from the review request text\ninstead. Worth refreshing or deleting before it is handed to anyone else.\n\nARCHITECTURE: preserved. Affected layers are 3/4 — internal/residentclient, the\nclient-side boundary for the local resident — and 7, the cmd/gs, cmd/gitseq-mcp\nand cmd/gitseq-github surfaces. I re-checked against this delta rather than\ncarrying the conclusion over; it is one line in a test file. Nothing in\ndocs/reference/architecture.md pins a submission deadline, a transport backstop,\nor where an adapter reads its configuration, and the page's rows for the three\nsurfaces describe what they compose rather than what they time out after.\nNothing changes what the kernel admits, what the fold decides, what any\nprojection says, or any dependency direction. architecture.md is correctly\nabsent from the set.\n\nSECURITY: no defect stands. Both defects I raised at the first head are fixed and\nre-proved here: the batch no longer tells an author a rerun is free when it would\nappend a duplicate durable act, and the derived backstop can no longer saturate\nto nothing and leave a call unbounded. The ordering regression that the second\nrepair introduced is fixed at the right place — the refusal gates attendAs, so a\nmisconfigured adapter never announces presence or takes a lease; I confirmed that\nwith the built binary, where the refusal is now the first line printed and the\nattach message is gone. Nothing in this change alters what is signed, admitted or\nattributed, and the environment variable carries the same authority as the flag\nit defaults for. The three hermeticity holes were gate-integrity rather than\nruntime, and all three are closed.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: nothing left to cut that would not weaken a condition. The AST\ngate is smaller and stronger than the runtime loop it backs. The one thing I\nwould still watch is the context-carrier pattern now living in two packages; a\nthird copy would argue for putting it beside ResolveSubmitDeadline in\nresidentclient, and that is a note for whoever adds the third, not a change to\nmake now.\n\nON CONDITION 6. The gate is green at this head on my run, and I have said\neverything I know about when it is not. Five runs between us at the previous\nhead, three green and two red, both reds in internal/app association and lineage\ntests that this lane does not touch and cannot reach — no residentclient in\ninternal/app's build graph, test deps included, and no changed path beneath it. I\ncould not prove the cause: the instrumented run that would have printed the\ndiscarded error came back green. The mechanism is consistent from source and no\nmore than consistent. That it is now a durable assert and a request of its own,\nrather than a sentence in a report that would be superseded with it, is the right\nplace for it to live.\n\nFour rounds is not a reason to approve, and neither is fatigue. I am approving\nbecause each thing I asked for is closed and I watched it close under my own\nmutations, and because what remains is a word count and a line number in a report\nwhose substance is true.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4c874727115391b301072725cbff44447021848b","binding":"assigned","head":"c92b20ccdb31c1db35112c17b8070fadbe948663","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:67c42123ac45eeb590c24747d9212b89516c0fab\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:46e1dcd4888166e1c7660cad46cfa0622a7ab3ab\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:67c42123ac45eeb590c24747d9212b89516c0fab","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}